Use Testcontainers. Spring Boot can take the connection details from the running container, so you never write the URL.

**Preferred: `@ServiceConnection`**

Add `spring-boot-testcontainers` as a test dependency. Then annotate the container field [S6]:

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	@ServiceConnection
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() { }
}
```

Spring Boot defines a `Neo4jConnectionDetails` bean from the container. Auto-configuration uses that bean, and it overrides any connection-related properties [S6].

For a relational database, `JdbcConnectionDetails` is matched on `JdbcDatabaseContainer` types. Flyway and Liquibase connection details are matched the same way. R2DBC has its own set of matched container types, including `PostgreSQLContainer` and `MySQLContainer` [S2]. Many other services are supported too, such as MongoDB, Kafka, Cassandra and Redis-style brokers like RabbitMQ [S2].

**Container as a Spring bean**

You can also declare the container as a bean in a `@TestConfiguration` and pull it in with `@Import`. The docs say this is often combined with service connection annotations [S4]. With this approach Spring manages the lifecycle. Containers start before other beans and stop after they are destroyed, which avoids connection errors on shutdown [S10].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {
	@Bean
	MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
		return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
	}
}
```

**Alternative: `@DynamicPropertySource`**

This is more verbose but more flexible. Use it when no service connection fits. It registers properties whose values are read from the container at runtime [S7]:

```java
@Container
static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

@DynamicPropertySource
static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
	registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
}
```

For a JDBC database, you would register `spring.datasource.url` the same way. The docs I found only show the Neo4j example, so that property name is my extrapolation.

**Module:** `@DataJpaTest` comes from the `spring-boot-data-jpa-test` module [S6]. The docs I searched don't give Maven or Gradle coordinates, so I can't show a dependency snippet.

**What it sets up** [S6]:
- It scans for `@Entity` classes and configures Spring Data JPA repositories.
- If an embedded database is on the classpath, it configures one.
- It sets `spring.jpa.show-sql` to `true`, so SQL queries are logged. You can turn this off with the annotation's `showSql` attribute.
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` if you need specific `@ConfigurationProperties` beans.
- Tests are transactional and roll back at the end of each test.
- You can inject a `TestEntityManager`, a test-oriented alternative to the standard `EntityManager`. A `JdbcTemplate` is also available [S1].

**Package names in Boot 4** [S1]:
```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jpa.test.autoconfigure.TestEntityManager;

@DataJpaTest
class MyRepositoryTests {
	@Autowired TestEntityManager entityManager;
	@Autowired UserRepository repository;
}
```

**Common adjustments:**
- To turn off the rollback, add `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` to the test class [S6].
- To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)`. That annotation is in `org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure` [S1].
- To use `TestEntityManager` in a test that isn't a `@DataJpaTest`, add `@AutoConfigureTestEntityManager`. Make sure the test runs in a transaction, for example with `@Transactional` [S1].

The docs also point to an appendix listing the exact auto-configurations that `@DataJpaTest` enables [S6]. That appendix wasn't in my search results.

Yes. Use `@DynamicPropertySource`. A static method of that type adds values to the Spring `Environment`, and the values can come from a running container [S1].

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() {
		// ...
	}

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}

}
```

The registry takes a property name and a supplier. The supplier is evaluated lazily, so the container's mapped port or URL is read when the property is needed. Neo4j beans in the application then connect to the container [S1].

- **Service connections:** The docs describe `@DynamicPropertySource` as a "slightly more verbose but more flexible alternative to service connections" [S1]. If the container type is supported, service connections need less code. My search didn't return their details, so check that section of the docs.
- **Lifecycle:** If beans depend on the container, the docs recommend declaring containers as Spring beans or importing them with `@ImportTestcontainers`. Containers managed by plain Testcontainers annotations have no guaranteed shutdown order relative to beans. A container can stop before the beans that use it are cleaned up, which can cause connection-loss exceptions [S2, S3].
- **Static properties:** If you only need fixed properties and not container-derived ones, `TestPropertyValues` can add them to an environment [S5].

Use Spring Framework's `@MockitoBean` annotation. It defines a Mockito mock for a bean in the `ApplicationContext`, so you don't need a configuration class [S1]. If you need a spy, `@MockitoSpyBean` does the same for a Mockito spy [S1].

Here is the documentation's example in a `@WebFluxTest` slice. The mock replaces `UserVehicleService`, and the controller under test gets the mock injected [S5]:

```java
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;

@WebFluxTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private WebTestClient webClient;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		this.webClient.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN).exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```

The import is `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean`. It comes from Spring Framework, not from a Spring Boot package [S5].

The Boot docs I could search only point to the Spring Framework documentation for more detail on these annotations [S1]. They don't cover details such as how a mock is matched to a bean or how it is reset between tests.

Use `@MockitoSpyBean`. It's a Spring Framework annotation that defines a Mockito spy for a bean in the `ApplicationContext` [S1]. It wraps the real bean, so the real behavior is kept and you can still verify calls.

The Spring Boot docs I can search only mention the annotation briefly. They point to the Spring Framework documentation for details, and that isn't in my search results [S1]. So I have no documented example for you. The code below is a sketch based on the `@MockitoBean` import path shown in the docs [S2]. I haven't confirmed the spy package or the details of how it behaves.

```java
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoSpyBean;
// verify(...) comes from org.mockito.Mockito

@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @MockitoSpyBean
    private MyService myService;   // the real bean, wrapped in a spy

    @Test
    void callsService() {
        // exercise the code under test, then:
        verify(myService).doSomething();
    }
}
```

The `@MockitoBean` example in the WebFlux section shows the pattern for fields: you annotate a field in the test class, and it comes from the `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito` package [S2].

Use **`@WebMvcTest`**. It comes from the `spring-boot-webmvc-test` module. It sets up only the Spring MVC infrastructure and auto-configures `MockMvc`. Because of that, you don't need a full HTTP server or the full application context. [S2]

**What it scans.** Scanning is limited to `@Controller`, `@ControllerAdvice`, `@JacksonComponent`, `Converter`, `Filter`, `HandlerInterceptor`, `WebMvcConfigurer` and similar web-layer beans. Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans are not scanned. [S2]

**Collaborators.** Controllers usually depend on other beans, so you typically limit the test to one controller and provide mocks with `@MockitoBean`. [S2]

```java
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.assertj.MockMvcTester;

@WebMvcTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN))
			.hasStatusOk()
			.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```
[S1]

**Related details**
- If AssertJ is available, `MockMvcTester` is auto-configured along with `MockMvc`. [S2]
- To use `RestTestClient` on top of Mock MVC, add `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient`. [S2]
- `@SpringBootTest` with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` also gives you `MockMvc`, but it loads a full web `ApplicationContext`. Only the embedded server is not started. [S3, S4]
- The docs recommend `@WebMvcTest` if you want to focus only on the web layer. [S5]

Use Spring REST Docs through `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. It comes from the `spring-boot-restdocs` module. It replaces the REST Docs JUnit extension and wires REST Docs into the auto-configured `MockMvc` bean [S3].

**Basic usage**

Add `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` to a `@WebMvcTest`. Then inject the mock MVC bean and apply `document(...)` to the result [S2]:

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
@AutoConfigureRestDocs
class MyUserDocumentationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@Test
	void listUsers() {
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/users").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN)).hasStatusOk()
			.apply(document("list-users"));
	}
}
```

`document` is statically imported from `MockMvcRestDocumentation`. The docs say `MockMvcTester` and plain `MockMvc` share the same underlying `MockMvc` instance, so any configuration applies to both [S2].

**Output directory and URIs**

Snippets go to `target/generated-snippets` with Maven or `build/generated-snippets` with Gradle by default. You can change that directory with `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. You can also use it to set the host, scheme and port that appear in documented URIs [S3].

**Generate snippets for every call**

Define a `RestDocumentationResultHandler` bean. The auto-configuration calls `alwaysDo` with it, so every `MockMvc` call generates the default snippets [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyResultHandlerConfiguration {

	@Bean
	public RestDocumentationResultHandler restDocumentation() {
		return MockMvcRestDocumentation.document("{method-name}");
	}
}
```

**Advanced configuration**

For more control than the annotation attributes give you, register a `RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer` bean. This example switches the snippet template format to Markdown [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRestDocsConfiguration implements RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer {

	@Override
	public void customize(MockMvcRestDocumentationConfigurer configurer) {
		configurer.snippets().withTemplateFormat(TemplateFormats.markdown());
	}
}
```

Use **`RestTestClient`**. The docs recommend it for tests that call a started server [S1].

Start the server on a random port with `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)`. Then add `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and autowire the client. It resolves relative URIs against the running server and has its own API for verifying responses [S1]:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

The imports are `org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and `org.springframework.test.web.servlet.client.RestTestClient` [S1].

**AssertJ style:** wrap the response spec with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)`, then use AssertJ assertions [S4]:

```java
ResponseSpec spec = restClient.get().uri("/").exchange();
RestTestClientResponse response = RestTestClientResponse.from(spec);
assertThat(response).hasStatusOk().bodyText().isEqualTo("Hello World");
```

**Alternatives:**
- **`WebTestClient`:** it needs `spring-webflux` on the classpath. Annotate the test with `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` (`org.springframework.boot.webtestclient.autoconfigure`). Its API is similar [S4].
- **`TestRestTemplate`:** annotate the test with `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate`. It also needs the `spring-boot-restclient` dependency [S5]. That dependency enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, declare it on the main classpath, not only the test classpath [S5].

**Other notes:**
- Use `@LocalServerPort` if you need the actual port [S1].
- Without a running server, the default mock environment lets you use `MockMvc`, `MockMvcTester`, `RestTestClient` or `WebTestClient` backed by MockMvc [S3].

Use `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)`. A free port is picked at random on each test run [S2]. By default `@SpringBootTest` doesn't start a server and uses a mock environment instead [S5].

You can inject the port that was chosen with `@LocalServerPort` [S2]. To make requests against the server, autowire a client:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

The client resolves relative URIs against the running server [S2].

Other client options:
- **`RestTestClient`:** annotate the test class with `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` [S2]. For AssertJ-style assertions, wrap the response with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)` [S3].
- **`WebTestClient`:** this needs `spring-webflux` on the classpath. Annotate the test class with `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` [S3].
- **`TestRestTemplate`:** annotate the test class with `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` [S4]. It needs the `spring-boot-restclient` dependency. That dependency enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, put the dependency on the main classpath and not only the test classpath [S4].

Two further points from the docs:
- If your application uses a separate port for the management server, `RANDOM_PORT` also starts it on its own random port [S1].
- Some sliced tests need `@AutoConfigureWebServer` to auto-configure an embedded web server [S3].

The helper class is `TestRestTemplate`. It's a convenience alternative to Spring's `RestTemplate` for integration tests, and it comes from the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module. You also need `spring-boot-restclient` on the classpath. Adding that dependency enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, put the dependency on the main classpath, not just the test classpath [S1].

**Setup against a running server**
- Use `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)` or `DEFINED_PORT`, and add `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate`. You can then inject a configured `TestRestTemplate` [S2].
- URLs without a host and port go to the embedded server [S2].
- You can customize it through the `RestTemplateBuilder` bean [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MySpringBootTests {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate template;

    @Test
    void testRequest() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = template.getForEntity("/example", String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

**Security and errors**
- You can get a vanilla template or one that sends Basic HTTP authentication with a username and password [S1].
- Either way, the template is fault tolerant. It doesn't throw exceptions on 4xx and 5xx responses. You check the returned `ResponseEntity` and its status code instead [S1].

**Redirects**
- The passages I found don't say how `TestRestTemplate` handles redirects.
- They do recommend Apache HTTP Client 5.1 or better. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures the client appropriately [S1].

**Alternatives**
- `RestTestClient` is the newer option. Use `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient`. It resolves relative links to the running server and has a fluent API for verifying responses [S5].
- For WebFlux, use `WebTestClient` [S1].

Use `@TestConfiguration`. It adds beans to your application's primary configuration. It doesn't replace that configuration.

**Nested class in the test**

A nested `@TestConfiguration` class is used in addition to your primary configuration. A nested regular `@Configuration` class would replace the primary configuration instead [S3].

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @TestConfiguration
    static class Extra {
        @Bean
        Clock fixedClock() {
            return Clock.fixed(Instant.EPOCH, ZoneOffset.UTC);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void exampleTest() { }
}
```

The Extra class and bean are my own illustration. The documentation passages don't include a nested example.

**Top-level class, imported where needed**

You can also put `@TestConfiguration` on a top-level class. This tells component scanning not to pick the class up. Without it, a configuration class written for specific tests can get picked up in every test. You then pull it in explicitly with `@Import` [S4]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestsConfiguration.class)
class MyTests {
    @Test
    void exampleTest() { }
}
```

**Things to know**
- An imported `@TestConfiguration` is processed before an inner-class one. It is also processed before any configuration found through component scanning. This only matters if you rely on bean overriding [S4].
- If you use `@ComponentScan` directly instead of `@SpringBootApplication`, register the `TypeExcludeFilter` on it [S4].
- Slice tests such as `@WebMvcTest` work the same way. `@Import` on the test class registers extra components, for example a `JacksonModule` [S6].
- Spring caches application contexts between tests. Tests that share the same configuration load the context only once [S3]. Test classes with different extra configuration get separate contexts.

**`@JdbcTest`** is the annotation you want. It comes from the `spring-boot-jdbc-test` module. It's for tests that only need a `DataSource` and don't use Spring Data JDBC [S1].

- **What it configures:** By default it sets up an in-memory embedded database and a `JdbcTemplate` [S1].
- **What it skips:** Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans aren't scanned. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S1].
- **Transactions:** Tests are transactional and roll back after each test by default. To turn that off, use `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` [S1].
- **Real database:** To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase` [S1].

```java
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.JdbcTest;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@JdbcTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyTransactionalTests {

}
```

Two related annotations cover other cases:
- `@DataJdbcTest` is for tests that use Spring Data JDBC repositories [S2].
- `@DataJpaTest` is the JPA slice. It also makes a `JdbcTemplate` available [S3].

Use `@JsonTest`. It's in `spring-boot-test-autoconfigure` and sets up only the JSON mapper, not the whole application context [S3].

**What it configures:** the available JSON library, which can be one of these [S3]:
- Jackson `JsonMapper`, plus any `@JacksonComponent` beans and Jackson `JacksonModule`s.
- The deprecated Jackson 2 `ObjectMapper`.
- `Gson`.
- `Jsonb`.

**Helpers:** `JacksonTester`, `GsonTester`, `JsonbTester` and `BasicJsonTester` (for Strings). They are AssertJ-based and work with JSONAssert and JsonPath. You can `@Autowired` them into the test class [S3].

```java
@JsonTest
class MyJsonTests {

	@Autowired
	private JacksonTester<VehicleDetails> json;

	@Test
	void serialize() throws Exception {
		VehicleDetails details = new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic");
		// Assert against a `.json` file in the same package as the test
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).isEqualToJson("expected.json");
		// Or use JSON path based assertions
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).hasJsonPathStringValue("@.make");
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).extractingJsonPathStringValue("@.make").isEqualTo("Honda");
	}

	@Test
	void deserialize() throws Exception {
		String content = "{\"make\":\"Ford\",\"model\":\"Focus\"}";
		assertThat(this.json.parse(content)).isEqualTo(new VehicleDetails("Ford", "Focus"));
		assertThat(this.json.parseObject(content).getMake()).isEqualTo("Ford");
	}
}
```
[S2]

**Notes:**
- To customize the auto-configuration, use `@AutoConfigureJsonTesters` [S3].
- You can use the helpers in plain unit tests without `@JsonTest`. In that case, call the helper's `initFields` in your `@BeforeEach` method [S2].
- Numeric JSON path values may not work with `isEqualTo`, depending on their type. Use `satisfies` instead [S2]:

```java
assertThat(this.json.write(value)).extractingJsonPathNumberValue("@.test.numberValue")
	.satisfies((number) -> assertThat(number.floatValue()).isCloseTo(0.15f, within(0.01f)));
```
[S1]

Add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` to the test class. By default, `@DataJpaTest` configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath [S7]. This annotation stops it from doing that. The documentation's example is:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests {

	// ...

}
```
[S6]

The passages I found don't say where the real database's connection details come from. They don't cover that setup, such as a `spring.datasource.*` configuration or a container.

The same annotation works for `@JdbcTest`, `@DataJdbcTest` and `@DataR2dbcTest` [S2, S5, S8].

`@DataJpaTest` tests are still transactional and roll back after each test by default [S7].

Use Spring Boot's `OutputCaptureExtension`. It's a JUnit extension that captures `System.out` and `System.err` [S1].

Add `@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)`. Then inject `CapturedOutput` into a test method or into the test class constructor [S1].

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;

import org.springframework.boot.test.system.CapturedOutput;
import org.springframework.boot.test.system.OutputCaptureExtension;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)
class MyOutputCaptureTests {

	@Test
	void testName(CapturedOutput output) {
		System.out.println("Hello World!");
		assertThat(output).contains("World");
	}

}
```

In the documented example, `CapturedOutput` is passed straight to AssertJ's `assertThat(output)` and checked with `contains` [S1].

Yes. Declare each container as a `@Bean` in a test configuration class, and Spring Boot manages it for you [S2].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {

	@Bean
	MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
		return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
	}

}
```

Import the configuration in any test that needs the container, and autowire the container if you need it [S2]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestConfiguration.class)
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MongoDBContainer mongo;
	...
}
```

**Service connections.** You'll usually combine this with `@ServiceConnection`, which configures the application's connection details from the container [S2].
- On a `@Bean` method, Boot picks the connection type from the method's return type. It doesn't call the method, to avoid eager initialization [S6].
- That works for typed containers such as `Neo4jContainer` or `RabbitMQContainer` [S6].
- With `GenericContainer`, Boot can't tell which image is used. You have to give a hint with `@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")` [S6].
- You can also use `name` to override the detected connection type, for example with a custom image from a private registry [S6].

```java
@Bean
@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
	return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7");
}
```

**Lifecycle benefits**
- Container beans are created and started before all other beans. They are stopped after all other beans are destroyed, so client beans can clean up while the container is still available [S3].
- A container bean is started once per application context in the TestContext Framework. It is stopped when that context shuts down [S1].
- One container instance is often reused across tests from multiple classes that share a cached context [S1].
- If Testcontainers manages the containers instead (static fields with its annotations), shutdown order isn't guaranteed. Containers can stop before the beans that depend on them, which can cause exceptions such as lost connections [S1].

The docs also mention `@ImportTestcontainers` as another option for importing container declarations. The passages I found don't show how to use it [S1].

Use `@ServiceConnection` on the Redis container. Spring Boot then creates a `DataRedisConnectionDetails` bean from it, so you don't need to set `spring.data.redis.*` properties yourself.

**What gets matched**

`DataRedisConnectionDetails` is created for containers of type `RedisContainer` or `RedisStackContainer`. It is also created for containers named `redis`, `redis/redis-stack` or `redis/redis-stack-server` [S1].

**Using a `GenericContainer`**

The docs only show a Redis example that uses `GenericContainer`, declared as a `@Bean`. Spring Boot can't read the image name from a `GenericContainer`, so you have to give it the name hint:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRedisConfiguration {

	@Bean
	@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
	public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
		return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7");
	}

}
```
[S9]

The `name` attribute also works for custom images. For `registry.mycompany.com/mirror/myredis`, use `@ServiceConnection(name="redis")` so that `DataRedisConnectionDetails` is created [S9].

**Typed containers**

