Use **`@RestClientTest`**. It comes from the `spring-boot-restclient-test` module, and its package is `org.springframework.boot.restclient.test.autoconfigure`. It auto-configures Jackson, GSON and Jsonb support. It also configures a `RestTemplateBuilder` and a `RestClient.Builder`, and adds `MockRestServiceServer` support [S1].

`@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans are not scanned. Name the beans under test with the `value` or `components` attribute. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` if you need `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S1].

```java
@RestClientTest(RemoteVehicleDetailsService.class)
class MyRestClientServiceTests {

	@Autowired
	private RemoteVehicleDetailsService service;

	@Autowired
	private MockRestServiceServer server;

	@Test
	void getVehicleDetailsWhenResultIsSuccessShouldReturnDetails() {
		this.server.expect(requestTo("https://example.com/greet/details"))
			.andRespond(withSuccess("hello", MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN));
		String greeting = this.service.callRestService();
		assertThat(greeting).isEqualTo("hello");
	}
}
```

How you write the URL in `expect(requestTo(...))` depends on how the client is built:
- **`RestClient.Builder`, or `RestTemplateBuilder` without `baseUri`:** use the full URI, as in the example above [S2].
- **`RestTemplateBuilder` with `baseUri(...)` called:** leave out the base URI. For example, use `requestTo("/greet/details")` [S1].
